Run GOOL scripts at the game's own 30 Hz step while physics runs every field.

With the 30 Hz gate open (0027) the outer loop runs once per NTSC field and
physics - scaled by measured frame time - is correct. The GOOL scripts are not:
everything GoolObjectUpdate (0x8001C718) does once per CALL was written for one
call per 34-tick frame, so in play animations, moving platforms and scripted
timers ran at twice their speed. The per-call work is the trans block, code
blocks resuming with a wait of 0 (0 and 1 are the same at 30 Hz), the stall
countdown at obj+228, and the pad read from Crash's update.

Nine recompile-time words (game.toml [[recompiler.patch]] c2-60-gool-*,
written by launcher/crash2launcher/recompprofile.py) make those depend on a
flag at sp+60 of the function's 64-byte frame - its unused padding word.
game.toml's mod_function_entry_funcs routes the function's entry to
c2_60_gool_entry here, which stores it: 0 on a physics-only field, otherwise
the crash object pointer (1 if none), which is the value the original code
compared the object against. With the flag never 0 the function is the
original, so the mode off is exact. Guest RAM keeps the disc's words; the text
image guard still validates the function and it stays native.

A loop is a script step once 34 ticks have passed since the last one: every
loop at 30 Hz, every other at 60. On top of the patched function:

  - 0x1F800054, the clamped ticks the GOOL opcodes at 0x8003A530 and 0x8003A758
    scale by, is rewritten after 0x80019F08 fills it (returns at 0x8001C2F0 and
    0x8001DF8C) with the ticks the script step covers, so scripted motion keeps
    its speed.
  - draw_count (0x80060944: texture animation, colour cycling, the
    (a + draw_count) % b opcode, flicker) is held back on physics-only fields,
    so it advances once per script step.
  - pad tapped words (0x80069920 + i*76 + 36) are hidden for a physics-only
    field and restored before the next read: the main loop tests START from
    them every loop (0x80011848) and would otherwise pause and unpause.
  - FIRST_FRAME (status_a 0x20), which the function clears after physics, is
    restored on a physics-only field, so an event-driven state change is still
    seen by the object's next script step.

Only paced once the entry hook has been called - the proof the generated code
is the patched build - so a project generated without the profile keeps the old
behaviour instead of getting the tick override alone. Off under netplay.
PSX_CRASH2_60FPS_SCRIPT_RATE=0 disables it for A/B.

Host writes go through memory_get_ram_ptr / memory_get_scratchpad_ptr: they
are the machine's own bookkeeping, must land in the load/store replay mode
(where psx_write_word does not write) and must not enter the lockstep or
data-shard recorders.

Heartbeat gains native_60fps_script_hz (~30 when paced right, at 30 or 60 FPS),
native_60fps_physics_fields and native_60fps_script_hooked. The debug
server's frame_rates gains native_60fps_script_steps (rate over its window)
for tuning/scripts/gameplay_smoke.py's expected_script_hz.

No file here is in codegen_hash_sources.cmake. The game.toml change moves the
overlay config hash (7399b41b -> d6362e32), so shards land in a new cache
directory; compile_overlays.py repopulates it from the stored captures.

