+/* Script rate: GOOL keeps the engine's 30 Hz step, physics runs every field.
+ *
+ * Measured in play with the gate open: animations, moving platforms and
+ * scripted timers ran at twice their speed while Crash's own movement was
+ * right. Everything GoolObjectUpdate (0x8001C718) does once per CALL was
+ * written for one call per 34-tick frame:
+ *
+ *   - the trans block runs on every call (0x8001C930);
+ *   - the code block resumes once frames_elapsed - stamp >= wait
+ *     (0x8001C974). A wait of 0 - common, because at 30 Hz 0 and 1 behave the
+ *     same - resumes on every call;
+ *   - the stall countdown, obj+228, ticks once per call (0x8001C8B8);
+ *   - the pad is read once per call, from Crash's update (0x8001C750).
+ *
+ * So at 60 Hz the scripts must keep their 30 Hz step while physics, already
+ * scaled by measured time, runs on every field. Nine recompile-time words in
+ * game.toml ([[recompiler.patch]] c2-60-gool-*) make those four things depend
+ * on a flag that c2_60_gool_entry - the function-entry hook game.toml routes
+ * 0x8001C718 to - stores at sp+60 of the function's 64-byte frame, its unused
+ * padding word, before the function runs. 0 means a physics-only field;
+ * anything else is the crash object pointer (1 if there is none), which is
+ * exactly what the original code compared each object against - so while the
+ * flag is never 0 the function IS the original. The trans save, 0x8001BFDC,
+ * physics and the render/bound path run on every field either way: the
+ * collision bound list is rebuilt every loop (0x8002CA98) and must stay whole.
+ *
+ * A field is a script step once a stock frame (34 ticks) has passed since the
+ * last one: every loop at 30 Hz, every other loop at 60, and straight away
+ * after a frame that needed two fields. Three more things the scripts read
+ * follow that step:
+ *
+ *   - the tick scale the GOOL opcodes use. 0x8003A530 (b + a*ticks >> 10) and
+ *     the angle seek at 0x8003A758 read 0x1F800054, which 0x80019F08 refills
+ *     from the draw buffer every loop. It is overwritten with the ticks the
+ *     script step really covers, so scripted motion keeps its speed;
+ *   - draw_count (0x80060944): world and object texture animation, colour
+ *     cycling, the (a + draw_count) % b opcode, flicker. The frame finalizer
