+ *     advances once per script step, as it did at 30 Hz;
+ *   - the pad's tapped bits. The pad read now runs on script steps only, but
+ *     the main loop tests START from them at 0x80011848 on every loop, so a
+ *     tap left standing through a physics-only field would pause and then
+ *     unpause. They are hidden for that field and put back just before the
+ *     next read, which files them as "tapped last time" - what a 30 Hz read
+ *     does - before computing new ones.
+ *
+ * And one flag: GoolObjectUpdate clears FIRST_FRAME (status_a 0x20) after
+ * physics. A state change an event delivers during a physics-only field would
+ * lose it before the object's scripts ever saw it, so on those fields the bit
+ * is put back.
+ *
+ * All of it is idle unless this mode is on, and exact at 30 Hz: every loop is
+ * then a script step, the flag is the crash pointer, and the scratchpad value
+ * written is the one 0x80019F08 just wrote. PSX_CRASH2_60FPS_SCRIPT_RATE=0
+ * turns it off for A/B - which brings the double speed back. */
